{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778480732343,"sessionID":"ses_1ea4b612affeSxP1xE8biNFmuV","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-853aa6ddf02a7300","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/tmp/slop-factory-work/echo-swap-29b861a4/CONVENTIONS.md","offset":1,"limit":2000},"output":"<path>/tmp/slop-factory-work/echo-swap-29b861a4/CONVENTIONS.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Slop Factory — Game Contract\n2: \n3: You are creating a small standalone browser game for the Slop Factory gallery. Your output is published unedited.\n4: \n5: ## Working directory\n6: \n7: You are running in an isolated working directory. You may read and write files here. You will be given the game concept in the prompt itself. Do **not** modify this `CONVENTIONS.md` file.\n8: \n9: ## Required output files\n10: \n11: Produce both of these files in the working directory:\n12: \n13: 1. **`index.html`** — a single self-contained HTML page that is the game.\n14: 2. **`meta.json`** — metadata describing the game, used by the gallery.\n15: \n16: You may write scratch files for your own use; only the two above are published.\n17: \n18: ## `meta.json` schema\n19: \n20: ```json\n21: {\n22:   \"title\": \"Neon Dodger\",\n23:   \"description\": \"Dodge falling neon blocks in a tiny ship. Stay alive as long as you can.\",\n24:   \"controls\": [\"Arrow keys to move\", \"Space to fire\"],\n25:   \"tags\": [\"arcade\", \"dodge\"]\n26: }\n27: ```\n28: \n29: Rules:\n30: - `title` — string, 3–80 characters, matches the game's display name.\n31: - `description` — string, 20–400 characters, one short paragraph.\n32: - `controls` — non-empty array of short strings, one per control.\n33: - `tags` — optional array of lowercase keywords.\n34: \n35: ## `index.html` requirements\n36: \n37: ### Self-contained\n38: - All CSS goes in a single `<style>` block in `<head>`.\n39: - All JS goes in a single `<script>` block at the end of `<body>`.\n40: - **No** external resources: no `<script src=\"https://...\">`, no `<link href=\"https://...\">`, no `<img src=\"https://...\">`, no CDN imports.\n41: - **No** network calls at runtime: no `fetch`, no `XMLHttpRequest`, no `import()`, no `WebSocket`.\n42: \n43: ### Required structure (in this order)\n44: \n45: ```html\n46: <!DOCTYPE html>\n47: <html lang=\"en\">\n48: <head>\n49:   <meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n50:   <title>YOUR TITLE</title>\n51:   <style>/* inline only */</style>\n52: </head>\n53: <body>\n54:   <header class=\"game-header\">\n55:     <h1>YOUR TITLE</h1>\n56:     <p class=\"game-description\">YOUR DESCRIPTION</p>\n57:     <ul class=\"game-controls\">\n58:       <li>YOUR CONTROL</li>\n59:     </ul>\n60:   </header>\n61:   <main>\n62:     <canvas id=\"game\" width=\"800\" height=\"600\"></canvas>\n63:     <button id=\"restart\" class=\"game-restart\" type=\"button\">Restart</button>\n64:   </main>\n65:   <script>/* inline only */</script>\n66: </body>\n67: </html>\n68: ```\n69: \n70: - Exactly one `<canvas id=\"game\">` (you may set width/height to other values; 800x600 is recommended).\n71: - Exactly one `<button id=\"restart\" class=\"game-restart\">` immediately after the canvas. Clicking it must fully reset the game (same code path as the R key). It must be visible and clickable at all times — not only on game-over.\n72: - The `<header>`'s `<h1>` text must match `meta.json.title` exactly.\n73: - The `<p class=\"game-description\">` text must match `meta.json.description` exactly.\n74: - The `<li>` items in `<ul class=\"game-controls\">` must match `meta.json.controls` in order.\n75: \n76: ### Behavior\n77: - The game must start rendering immediately on page load (no \"click to start\" required).\n78: - Use keyboard and/or mouse input.\n79: - The script must not throw any uncaught exception in the first 5 seconds.\n80: - If you use `localStorage` or `sessionStorage`, all keys must start with the prefix `slop-factory:echo-swap:`.\n81: \n82: ### Style\n83: - Dark background (the gallery is dark — recommend `#0a0a0f` or similar).\n84: - High-contrast neon accents (e.g. cyan `#7df9ff`, magenta `#ff00aa`, yellow `#ffd23f`).\n85: - The `<header>` is shown above the canvas — keep its styling minimal and let the canvas content shine.\n86: \n87: ## What makes a tiny canvas game feel good\n88: \n89: Aim for these qualities in every game you produce:\n90: \n91: 1. **Immediate input response** — every keystroke or click produces visible action within one frame. Never make the player wait.\n92: 2. **Clear feedback** — the player always knows when something happened. Use color flashes, particles, brief screen shake, score popups, or sound on every meaningful event (hit, score, miss, death).\n93: 3. **Win/lose state** — the game has a clear end condition. On lose, draw \"Game Over\" + final score + restart instructions on the canvas. On win, celebrate (color burst, \"You Win!\" overlay).\n94: 4. **Restart without page reload** — both pressing R AND clicking the `<button id=\"restart\">` reset the game cleanly via the same code path. Mention both in `controls` (e.g. \"Press R or click Restart\"). On restart you MUST `cancelAnimationFrame` any pending loop before starting a new one (otherwise loops stack on each restart and the game eventually slows to a crawl).\n95: 5. **Progressive difficulty** — start gentle so the player can learn, then ramp up. Don't dump them into chaos at t=0.\n96: 6. **No dead time** — something interesting on screen at all times. Idle background animation (stars, gradient pulse, particles) is fine.\n97: 7. **Persistent score / progress UI** — drawn on the canvas at the top corner, always visible.\n98: 8. **Audio (optional but encouraged)** — generate tones with `OscillatorNode` (no external assets). **Do not auto-start audio on page load** — browsers block this and it will throw. Initialize the `AudioContext` lazily on the first keypress or click. Wrap audio code in try/catch.\n99: \n100: ## Playable Contract — non-negotiable\n101: \n102: These are not aspirations. A game that violates any of these is broken even if it runs without errors. After your implementation finishes, the validator loads the page in a real browser and runs an 8-second playtest with synthetic input — failures here are reported back to you and must be fixed before the game ships.\n103: \n104: ### 1. The player can lose\n105: \n106: There must be a collision check (or other end condition) that fires `gameOver` and stops the active gameplay loop. \"Game runs forever, no death\" is broken. Examples that have failed in the past: collision check compared a value against itself; \"spike\" collision only clamped position instead of killing the player; obstacles never reached the player.\n107: \n108: ### 2. The player can score\n109: \n110: The score must increase on a **discrete event** (a survived obstacle, a collected pickup, an enemy destroyed, a beat hit). \"Score = elapsed seconds\" is allowed but weak. Score must be drawn on the canvas at all times.\n111: \n112: ### 3. The first 3 seconds are survivable with no input\n113: \n114: Define a `SAFE_RADIUS` (e.g. 200px) around the player's starting position. When spawning hazards, **reject any spawn that is within `SAFE_RADIUS` of the player AND would reach them within 3 seconds at current hazard speed**. Pre-spawning hazards on top of the player at t=0 has caused frame-1 deaths in past games. Don't do it.\n115: \n116: ### 4. The control actually changes the outcome\n117: \n118: Whatever the player presses must, in the next frame, change a value that affects collision or score. If a control is purely cosmetic (e.g. \"stretch horizontally\" but the gaps are vertical), the game is fundamentally broken even if it runs. After writing, **re-read the game's concept and trace one frame**: does the input do what the concept claims?\n119: \n120: ### 5. dt-based physics, not per-frame constants\n121: \n122: All movement and timing must scale by delta time (seconds since last frame). Per-frame constants (`x += 5` every frame) make games run 2.4× faster on a 144Hz display.\n123: \n124: ```js\n125: let lastTime = performance.now();\n126: function loop(now) {\n127:   const dt = Math.min((now - lastTime) / 1000, 0.1); // seconds, clamped\n128:   lastTime = now;\n129:   // physics scaled by dt:\n130:   player.x += player.vx * dt;\n131:   enemy.y  += enemy.vy  * dt;\n132:   spawnTimer -= dt;\n133:   // ...\n134:   requestAnimationFrame(loop);\n135: }\n136: requestAnimationFrame(loop);\n137: ```\n138: \n139: ### 6. Restart fully resets AND cancels the prior loop\n140: \n141: Both R and the Restart button must call the same reset function. That function MUST `cancelAnimationFrame(loopId)` the previous loop before scheduling a new one — otherwise loops stack on each restart and the game slows to a crawl over time.\n142: \n143: ```js\n144: let loopId = null;\n145: function startLoop() {\n146:   if (loopId !== null) cancelAnimationFrame(loopId);\n147:   function tick(now) { /* ... */ loopId = requestAnimationFrame(tick); }\n148:   loopId = requestAnimationFrame(tick);\n149: }\n150: ```\n151: \n152: ### 7. Game-over overlay shows score and restart instructions\n153: \n154: When the player dies, draw on the canvas: \"Game Over\", final score, current high score (if you persist one), and \"Press R or click Restart\". Do not rely on the HTML restart button alone — players need to know they can restart.\n155: \n156: ## Observable State Contract — required for playtest\n157: \n158: In addition to the rules above, your game **must** expose its current state on `window.__game` so the automated playtest can observe what the player would observe. A game that does not expose this object is rejected.\n159: \n160: ```js\n161: window.__game = {\n162:   state: \"menu\",        // \"menu\" | \"playing\" | \"gameover\" — exact strings, no others\n163:   score: 0,             // current score, number\n164:   player: {\n165:     x: 400,             // player x in canvas coordinates (0 = left)\n166:     y: 300,             // player y in canvas coordinates (0 = top)\n167:     visible: true,      // true if the player is currently rendered inside the canvas viewport\n168:   },\n169:   objective: \"Dodge falling neon blocks. Survive as long as possible.\", // 10-200 chars, one sentence\n170: };\n171: ```\n172: \n173: Rules:\n174: - The object must exist from the very first frame, before any input.\n175: - Update `state`, `score`, `player.x`, `player.y`, and `player.visible` whenever they change. The simplest approach is to update them at the end of your render/update step each frame.\n176: - `state` must only ever be one of the three exact strings. No other values.\n177: - `player.visible` is critical. **If your camera follows the player, the camera math must keep `player.visible` = true at all times.** A camera that lags the player off-screen is the most common failure here.\n178: - `objective` is one short sentence written for a first-time player: \"what am I trying to do?\" — derivable from observing the game alone, not from reading the meta.\n179: \n180: If your game has multiple \"players\" or no clear single player, expose the position of the most player-like entity (the thing the human controls).\n181: \n182: ## What the validator checks\n183: \n184: Your output is checked programmatically. Failure feeds the error report back to you for correction.\n185: \n186: 1. Both `index.html` and `meta.json` exist.\n187: 2. `meta.json` parses as JSON and matches the schema above.\n188: 3. `index.html` parses, has exactly one `<canvas id=\"game\">`, exactly one `<button id=\"restart\">` after it, and the `<header>` text matches `meta.json`.\n189: 4. No external URLs in `<script src=>`, `<link href=>`, `<img src=>` (regex scan).\n190: 5. Headless Chromium loads the page; no `console.error` or `pageerror` events in 5 seconds.\n191: 6. Canvas has non-blank pixels after 3 seconds (game is actually drawing something).\n192: 7. **Observable contract is present**: `window.__game` exists with the exact shape above, from the first frame.\n193: 8. **Playtest invariants** hold during ~8 seconds of synthetic key input (arrow keys, WASD, space):\n194:    - `state` only takes the values `\"menu\" | \"playing\" | \"gameover\"`.\n195:    - `player.visible` is true for ≥95% of polled frames.\n196:    - Either `score` changes at least once during play, or `state` reaches `\"gameover\"`. (A game where neither happens has no progress signal — broken even if it runs.)\n197: \n198: ## Tips\n199: \n200: - Keep the implementation simple and robust over flashy and fragile.\n201: - Test mentally: imagine loading the page, pressing keys, watching for 5 seconds — would anything throw?\n202: - Use `requestAnimationFrame` for the loop. Wrap the loop body in a try/catch so a stray bug doesn't crash the whole thing.\n\n(End of file - total 202 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Slop Factory — Game Contract\n\nYou are creating a small standalone browser game for the Slop Factory gallery. Your output is published unedited.\n\n## Working directory\n\nYou are running in an isolated working directory. You may read and write files here. You will be given the game concept in the prompt itself. Do **not** modify this `CONVENTIONS.md` file.\n\n## Required output files\n\nProduce both of these files in the working directory:\n\n1. **`index.html`** — a single self-contained HTML page that is the game.\n2. **`meta.json`** — metadata describing the game, used by the gallery.\n\nYou may write scratch files for your own use; only the two above are published.\n\n## `meta.json` schema\n\n```json","truncated":false,"loaded":[]},"title":"tmp/slop-factory-work/echo-swap-29b861a4/CONVENTIONS.md","time":{"start":1778480732322,"end":1778480732341}},"id":"prt_e15b653e8001g34otvcZNZmVKV","sessionID":"ses_1ea4b612affeSxP1xE8biNFmuV","messageID":"msg_e15b6309b001OD6nGKJygnGXs6"}}
